  "text_md": "Whether a daw sit, or whether a daw fly.\nTher was never no better in Crystyant^.\nPERLID. Ornamented with pearls ; studded\ntional strength.\nseaboorde. Lambnrde's Perambulation, 1596, p. 357,\nPERSEVER. To persevere. Shak.\nto dressing the heron.\nPERRIWINKLE. A periwig. Stubbe. PERRONENDERE. A pardoner. Heame.\ngenuine old word.\n- PERS. (1) Persia. We woot bothe bi story and vers That the kyndam of Grace and Pera Were hede kyngus in forme tide.\n;\nPERPLANTED. Planted securely.\nFore as I sey so schall it be. MS.Ashmole Cl>\nRequlrynge theim as his especiall truste and confidence was perplanted in the hope of their fidelite, that they would occurrc and mete hym by the vraye with all diligent preparacion.\nPERMANSIE. Magic ; necromancy.\nAnd many a perZtdgarnement\nmuch used for stone shot.\nPERPETUANA. A kind of glossy cloth, gene-\n- (2) A httle cur dog. North.\nAnd observe rightly what's the proper end.\nwith any ornaments.\nPERRIER. A kind of short mortar, formerly\nPERRYE. (1) A squall.\nPERSE. Equality. (A.-N.)\nPERSORE. A piercing-iron.\nPERLESY. A pleurisy.\n- (3) Precious stones\n- (5) A wooden frame against which sawn timber is set up to dry. East.\nPERRY-DANCERS. The aurora borealis. East\nby Andrew Borde, Brit. Bibl. iv. 24.\n- Pilkington'a Workt, p. 56.\nGlimpses, 1659, p. 73.\nof peas, onions, and spices.\nMy saied lorde of Winchester saied unto the kyng that the kyng his father, so visited with sitkenesse, was not personable. Hall, Henry VI. f. 13.\nPERSONE. A man. Generally, a man of\nPERSAUNT. Piercing. {A.-N.)\nPERSEL. Parsley. Pegge.\njewels. {A.-N.)\nBrome'a Songs, 1661, p. 182.\nCurso)- Mundi, MS. Coll. Trin. Cantab, f. 132.\nLydgate, MS. Sue. Antiq. 134, f. 23.\nIt happened Harold his sonne to arrive at Pountiou against his will, by occasion of a sudden perry, or contrarie winde, that arose while he was on\nTarlton'a Jeats, 1611.\nFor thy perseynt charit^.\nAnd smyttSs hym als it were with a perlesy, that\nof golde, in the branches of whilke ther were many maners of fewles. MS. Lincoln A. i. 17, f. 25.\nPERKIN. Water cyder.\nBut these tender pemels must have one gown for the day, another for the night.\nPERQUIRE. To search into. Clobery's Divine",
